<div id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The problem is equivalent to finding the largest 2-colorable subgraph of a given graph and is known as the partial 2-coloring problem. The work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">GLR19</a>]</cite> studies the problem in the Feige-Kilian semi-random model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">FK01</a>]</cite>, where a 2-colorable graph of size <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(1-\varepsilon\right)n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(1-\varepsilon\right)n</annotation></semantics></math> is planted. They give an algorithm that outputs a set <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lvert\mathcal{S^{\prime}}\right\rvert\geqslant\left(1-\varepsilon c/p^{2}\right)n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lvert\mathcal{S^{\prime}}\right\rvert\geqslant\left(1-\varepsilon c/p^{2}\right)n</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=\Omega\left(\sqrt{\log n/n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=\Omega\left(\sqrt{\log n/n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\leqslant p^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>⩽</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\leqslant p^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is a positive constant.
Their algorithm is a partial recovery algorithm and works for the regimes when <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is small.
