<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Characterizing dual variables through optimality conditions:</h5>

<div id="S1.SS4.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
Lemma 2.3
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.SSS2.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.SSS2.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These impose a “wish list” of desired conditions, which can be used to characterize our dual variables</p>
--
<p id="S1.SS4.SSS3.Px1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Now, all we need is a setting of <math id="S1.SS4.SSS3.Px1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>’s such that the system of equations is satisfied. However, this will still not be enough. We note that if this system of equations were to have a solution, we would have set some of these <math id="S1.SS4.SSS3.Px1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> variables to non-zero values. Therefore our equation <a href="#S1.E10" title="In Showing optimality of dual solution through weakly characterized dual variables ‣ 1.4.2 Traditional SDP Analysis ‣ 1.4 Proof Overview ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Exact recovery algorithm for Planted Bipartite Graph in Semi-random Graphs" class="ltx_ref">(<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></span>)</a> would now need to be modified to showing that <math id="S1.SS4.SSS3.Px1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\min}\left(A\right)+d+\lambda_{\min}\left(B\right)\geqslant 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\min}\left(A\right)+d+\lambda_{\min}\left(B\right)\geqslant 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.SSS3.Px1.p7" class="ltx_para">
Lemma 22.
</div>
</section>
</section>
--
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Structure of threshold rank/spectral embedding vectors.</h5>

<div id="S1.SS4.SSS4.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
Lemma 28.
Lemma 29.
We choose our parameters such that the vectors in <math id="S1.SS4.SSS4.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{-\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{-\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> are orthogonal to <math id="S1.SS4.SSS4.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbm{1}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{1}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. For the embedding vectors, this translates to saying that <math id="S1.SS4.SSS4.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i\in S}\mathbf{w}^{\left(i\right)}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i\in S}\mathbf{w}^{\left(i\right)}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.SSS4.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.SSS4.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Towards bounding the second term, we use these <span id="S1.SS4.SSS4.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">spectral embedding vectors</span>. The spectral embedding vectors are isotropic for <math id="S1.SS4.SSS4.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=1</annotation></semantics></math> (already where we can easily show that equation <a href="#S1.E17" title="In An LP formulation and Farkas Lemma based approach. ‣ 1.4.4 Setting of dual variables ‣ 1.4 Proof Overview ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Exact recovery algorithm for Planted Bipartite Graph in Semi-random Graphs" class="ltx_ref">(<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></span>)</a> does not hold and we are done).
However, for <math id="S1.SS4.SSS4.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S1.SS4.SSS4.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in N(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in N(j)</annotation></semantics></math> (and corresponding embedding vectors) being sampled randomly as per <math id="S1.SS4.SSS4.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{n,p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{n,p}</annotation></semantics></math> distribution.
Lemma 31.
<table id="S1.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
--
<p id="S1.SS4.SSS4.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Now, we look at two cases; the first case where the negative terms dominate the summand in equation <a href="#S1.E17" title="In An LP formulation and Farkas Lemma based approach. ‣ 1.4.4 Setting of dual variables ‣ 1.4 Proof Overview ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Exact recovery algorithm for Planted Bipartite Graph in Semi-random Graphs" class="ltx_ref">(<span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></span>)</a>, then we use the eigenvector structure that <math id="S1.SS4.SSS4.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lVert\mathbf{w}^{\left(i\right)}\right\rVert\leqslant 2/\sqrt{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lVert\mathbf{w}^{\left(i\right)}\right\rVert\leqslant 2/\sqrt{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and we are done; for the other case where the positive terms dominate, we relate the positive terms to the negative terms again using the bound we obtained from the eigenvector structure <math id="S1.SS4.SSS4.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lVert\sum_{i\in N(j)}\mathbf{w}^{\left(i\right)}\right\rVert=\mathcal{O}_{p}\left(\sqrt{\log k}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lVert\sum_{i\in N(j)}\mathbf{w}^{\left(i\right)}\right\rVert=\mathcal{O}_{p}\left(\sqrt{\log k}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.SSS4.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
Lemma 34.
Lemma 2.3
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS4.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
Low degree regimes

<div id="S1.SS4.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The discussion above about SDP holds only where <math id="S1.SS4.SSS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=\gamma pk" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=\gamma pk</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.SS4.SSS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\geqslant 2/3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\geqslant 2/3</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that this covers our interesting regimes when <math id="S1.SS4.SSS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\approx pk" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\approx pk</annotation></semantics></math> where the problem is non-trivial. The other case where <math id="S1.SS4.SSS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leqslant 2pk/3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leqslant 2pk/3</annotation></semantics></math>, can actually be trivially solved for <math id="S1.SS4.SSS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=\Omega_{p}\left(\sqrt{n\log n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=\Omega_{p}\left(\sqrt{n\log n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> using a degree counting argument along the lines of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kuc95</a>]</cite> as we discuss below.</p>
--
</div>
</figure>
<div id="Thmtheorem16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
Lemma 16
<div id="Thmtheorem16.p1" class="ltx_para">
Low degree regimes
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.SSS5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S1.SS4.SSS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
Theorem 4.4
<table id="S1.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
--
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\ProbOp\left[d(v)\geqslant d+p(n-k)+\sqrt{6pn\log n}\right]\leqslant\frac{1}{n}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">ℙ</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ProbOp\left[d(v)\geqslant d+p(n-k)+\sqrt{6pn\log n}\right]\leqslant\frac{1}{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
